<!--
<!DOCTYPE MediaCodecs [
<!ELEMENT MediaCodecs (Decoders,Encoders)>
<!ELEMENT Decoders (MediaCodec*)>
<!ELEMENT Encoders (MediaCodec*)>
<!ELEMENT MediaCodec (Type*,Quirk*)>
<!ATTLIST MediaCodec name CDATA #REQUIRED>
<!ATTLIST MediaCodec type CDATA>
<!ELEMENT Type EMPTY>
<!ATTLIST Type name CDATA #REQUIRED>
<!ELEMENT Quirk EMPTY>
<!ATTLIST Quirk name CDATA #REQUIRED>
]>

There's a simple and a complex syntax to declare the availability of a
media codec:

A codec that properly follows the OpenMax spec and therefore doesn't have any
quirks and that only supports a single content type can be declared like so:

    <MediaCodec name="OMX.foo.bar" type="something/interesting" />

If a codec has quirks OR supports multiple content types, the following syntax
can be used:

    <MediaCodec name="OMX.foo.bar" >
        <Type name="something/interesting" />
        <Type name="something/else" />
        ...
        <Quirk name="requires-allocate-on-input-ports" />
        <Quirk name="requires-allocate-on-output-ports" />
        <Quirk name="output-buffers-are-unreadable" />
    </MediaCodec>

Only the three quirks included above are recognized at this point:

"requires-allocate-on-input-ports"
    must be advertised if the component does not properly support specification
    of input buffers using the OMX_UseBuffer(...) API but instead requires
    OMX_AllocateBuffer to be used.

"requires-allocate-on-output-ports"
    must be advertised if the component does not properly support specification
    of output buffers using the OMX_UseBuffer(...) API but instead requires
    OMX_AllocateBuffer to be used.

"output-buffers-are-unreadable"
    must be advertised if the emitted output buffers of a decoder component
    are not readable, i.e. use a custom format even though abusing one of
    the official OMX colorspace constants.
    Clients of such decoders will not be able to access the decoded data,
    naturally making the component much less useful. The only use for
    a component with this quirk is to render the output to the screen.
    Audio decoders MUST NOT advertise this quirk.
    Video decoders that advertise this quirk must be accompanied by a
    corresponding color space converter for thumbnail extraction,
    matching surfaceflinger support that can render the custom format to
    a texture and possibly other code, so just DON'T USE THIS QUIRK.

-->

<MediaCodecs>
    <Settings>
        <Setting name="max-video-encoder-input-buffers" value="6" />
    </Settings>
    <Decoders>
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.mp4.decode" type="video/mp4v-es" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="size" min="32x32" max="1920x1088" />
            <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
            <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
            <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="244800" />
            <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-10000000" />
            <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
        </MediaCodec>
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.h263.decode" type="video/3gpp" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="size" min="32x32" max="720x576" />
            <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
            <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
        </MediaCodec>
        <!-- mocha note: libnvomx.so does contain a non-secure OMX.Nvidia.h264.decode,
             but that 2014 vendor blob is incompatible with the Android 14 ACodec
             native buffer ABI (setPortMode to DynamicANWBuffer fails outright), and in
             testing the picture never advanced while the decoder spun.
             Only the secure version (for DRM) is kept here; ordinary video goes to the
             software decoders in media_codecs_google_video.xml and plays fine with the
             SoftwareRenderer gralloc fallback (see the frameworks/av patches). -->
        <!-- mocha 2026-11-20 (conclusion): the non-secure hardware decoder is **gone for
             good**, do not add it back. Measured chain: the device has no
             /dev/nvhost-msdec|vde, and drivers/video/tegra/host/ only has
             msenc/vi/vic03/isp - no decoder engine driver - so ACodec fails with
             ERROR(0x80001000) and ExoPlayer reports "Decoder failed" (black screen and
             an error). The blob's hardware decode cannot initialise on this kernel at
             all, independent of ACodec/metadata negotiation (those patches fixed crashes,
             not decodability). H.264 here can only use OMX.google.h264.decoder. -->
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.h264.decode.secure" type="video/avc" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="size" min="32x32" max="3840x2176" />
            <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
            <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
            <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="783360" />
            <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-62500000" />
            <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
            <Feature name="secure-playback" required="true" />
        </MediaCodec>
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.vp8.decode" type="video/x-vnd.on2.vp8" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="size" min="32x32" max="3840x2176" />
            <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
            <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
            <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="783360" />
            <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-62500000" />
            <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
        </MediaCodec>
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.h265.decode" type="video/hevc" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="size" min="32x32" max="1920x1088" />
            <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
            <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
            <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="783360" />
            <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-10000000" />
            <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
        </MediaCodec>
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.mpeg2v.decode" type="video/mpeg2" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="size" min="32x32" max="3840x2176" />
            <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
            <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
            <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="783360" />
            <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-62500000" />
            <Feature name="adaptive-playback" />
        </MediaCodec>

        <!-- mocha fix: this was declared as audio/mpeg-L2, which Android 14's
             MediaCodecsXmlParser rejects, so startup logged "OMX.Nvidia.mp3.decoder
             does not support any media types" and the component was effectively dead.
             MP3 is audio/mpeg. -->
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.mp3.decoder" type="audio/mpeg" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="channel-count" max="2" />
            <Limit name="sample-rate" ranges="16000,22050,24000,32000,44100,48000" />
            <Limit name="bitrate" range="8000-320000" />
        </MediaCodec>
    </Decoders>

    <Encoders>
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.h264.encoder" type="video/avc" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="size" min="48x48" max="3840x2176" />
            <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
            <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
            <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="783360" />
            <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-50000000" />
        </MediaCodec>
        <MediaCodec name="OMX.Nvidia.vp8.encoder" type="video/x-vnd.on2.vp8" rank="0x300" >
            <Limit name="size" min="48x48" max="3840x2176" />
            <Limit name="alignment" value="2x2" />
            <Limit name="block-size" value="16x16" />
            <Limit name="blocks-per-second" min="1" max="783360" />
            <Limit name="bitrate" range="1-50000000" />
        </MediaCodec>
    </Encoders>

    <!-- Diagnostic: the Codec2 software audio decoders have been observed to
         accept a track and then never deliver a single output buffer (decoder
         created, 0 CPU time in media.swcodec, NuPlayer stuck waiting for the
         first decoded frame, AudioTrack never opened - silence). Pushing them
         behind the OMX.google software decoders routes audio through the legacy
         OMX audio path instead; if sound comes back, the fault is in the C2
         audio components. decimal rank on purpose: OmxInfoBuilder parses the
         attribute with sscanf("%u"), so hex silently fails. -->
    <Decoders>
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.mp3.decoder" update="true" rank="768" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.vorbis.decoder" update="true" rank="768" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.aac.decoder" update="true" rank="768" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.flac.decoder" update="true" rank="768" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.opus.decoder" update="true" rank="768" />
        <!-- [2026-11-22 music hang] This one was missed: FLACExtractor emits audio/raw
         * PCM for .flac, so NuPlayer picks c2.android.raw.decoder (trait rank 8,
         * visible in dumpsys media.player), which likewise swallows the track and never
         * emits a frame - NPDecoder's looper never drains, NuPlayer in mediaserver hangs
         * from then on, and every later MediaPlayer playback (music/video/ringtones)
         * advances state with no sound. Lower it to 768 so OMX.google.raw.decoder (rank
         * 0x10) handles audio/raw - same cause and same symptom as the entries above. -->
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.raw.decoder" update="true" rank="768" />
    </Decoders>

    <!-- The Codec2 software VIDEO decoders are healthy (unlike the audio ones)
         and handle 10-bit HEVC correctly, which OMX.google.hevc does not: its
         SoftwareRenderer fails lockYCbCr with ENOSYS on 10-bit YUV and falls
         back to an 8-bit YV12 layout, rendering near-black. Give them the top
         rank so video prefers C2 over OMX.google (property sw-other rank 100)
         and OMX.Nvidia (0x300). Audio stays OMX-first via the block above.
         Decimal rank on purpose: the parser uses sscanf("%u"). -->
    <Decoders>
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.avc.decoder" update="true" rank="8" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.hevc.decoder" update="true" rank="8" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.vp8.decoder" update="true" rank="8" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.vp9.decoder" update="true" rank="8" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.mpeg4.decoder" update="true" rank="8" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.h263.decoder" update="true" rank="8" />
        <MediaCodec name="c2.android.av1.decoder" update="true" rank="8" />
    </Decoders>

    <Include href="media_codecs_google_audio.xml" />
    <Include href="media_codecs_google_video.xml" />
</MediaCodecs>
